      "value": "@{62b61f8ec938a62475e4cd14|full_name} I've tried on both, multiplicative mode being the one that should work. I initially tried on realistic lighting before double checking on flat lighting. This issue occurs on both. In both cases, the matcap becomes too bright, seemingly due to how it reacts with realtime point/spotlight light sources.\n\nIf this is some issue with the shader's ordering structure, it may also be the cause of why light ramps are not giving 'expected results' when not set to flat on a matcap shader\n\nHere is the world I used, as it has a point light with a noticeable tint: https://vrchat.com/home/world/wrld_3d3232cf-e375-4c9b-a78c-f68b52f7d1e8/info\n\nHere is an avatar with flat lighting: https://vrchat.com/home/avatar/avtr_2c5c55a4-a67a-4d94-8b0f-7fceb1b697cf\n\nHere is the avatar in the image: https://vrchat.com/home/avatar/avtr_6bc02d21-7e84-41a0-87d2-32b141ca645a\n\nAnd in every instance, the lighting from point lights is far more extreme than a light from directional/baked lights.\n\nFor more proof, I've even had a version of the map that replaces the campfire's point light with a directional light, which should be all accounts be even brighter.\n\nPictured is the same light strength/color, affecting the same shader. the slime in both images is set to black.\n\nTo prove the lighting should be the same. the hat, using toon standard in both images, is actually nearly identical in lighting. With the directional light image on the left actually being a bit brighter/more saturated than the one on the right when comparing the colors side by side. The matcap does not act the same.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
